<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tablet of Shamash</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">9th century BCE</span> · Also known as Sun-god Tablet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Tablet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utu">Shamash</a> is a carved stone tablet found at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iraq">Iraq</a> in 1881 and now in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-museum">British Museum</a>. Made in the reign of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabu-apla-iddina">Nabu-apla-iddina</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> in the 9th century BCE, it records how the king restored the cult image of the sun god Shamash after it had been lost. Its relief shows the king being led before the enthroned god.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tablet-of-shamash.jpg" alt="Tablet of Shamash" width="320" height="222" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Natritmeyer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tablet_of_shamash_impression.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
